        title: "Project: sugarcane-cv — nine CV and audio detection problems at a Thai sugar mill",
        content: "sugarcane-cv collects nine detection problems from one real project at a Thai sugar mill, using CCTV cameras, a webcam at the weighbridge and a microphone at the tipping bay. The repository ships training code and core algorithms only: no model files and no customer images. (1) Dust opacity at tipping: LR-ASPP segmentation plus a density head at threshold 0.29, followed by a hand-written zone-evidence veto that weights each bay percentage by the share of pixels that look like airborne dust; the README states plainly that the scored test set contains no real photograph with dust in it, all positives are AI-generated, so the recall figures test pipeline logic on synthetic input and are not field accuracy. (2) Burnt vs fresh cane: EfficientNet-B0 at 384px, 6 epochs, 5-fold cross-validation over 8,612 real CCTV frames, 4 classes, 6 days, 2 cameras, from the public Roboflow aimlsugarcane dataset (CC BY 4.0); mean recall 0.873 +/-0.117, precision 0.843 +/-0.108, accuracy 0.9586 +/-0.0069, worst fold recall 0.711, mean F1 0.854 against a camera-only baseline of 0.417 — but class and camera are almost fully entangled (94% of burnt from one camera, 79% of fresh from the other), so per-camera recall is 0.944 and 0.415, and the lower number is called the honest one. (3) Mixed burnt cane: no model, a bright-stalk percentage rule (burnt piles 0.6-6%, fresh piles 19-21% even in shade); black% was rejected because shaded fresh cane read 36.6% dark against 43-60% for burnt. (4) Dirty area: inconclusive — 65 original images expanded to 158 by Roboflow, 54 real trucks, the sugar-cane class had zero annotations, the published split leaked, and the cross-camera score of 0.842 exactly equals the majority-class baseline. (5) Acoustic contaminants: log-mel CNN on impacts generated with Stable Audio Open and mixed onto real tipping-bay background at SNR +12 to -12 dB; on real unseen audio the 0.5 s window gives sand recall 0.94 at precision 1.00 and 2.2 false alarms per minute, versus 7.6 for an energy rule. (6) Cane flow on the conveyor: self-training leaf segmentation at 69.9 fps. (7) Thai plate OCR: two independent readers (ONNX plus OpenCV, and a pure-CV template one that runs on a Raspberry Pi); on a 60-image hard synthetic set the tuned reader answers 51/60, 51 correct, 0 wrong, 9 referred to a human, median 182 ms down from 2,236 ms. (8) Cane-on-truck: no model, edge and motion cues at 3.9 ms per frame on CPU. (9) Stalk segmentation: frozen DINOv2 backbone plus a two-layer head on 31 hand-labelled images. Modules live under src/dust_opacity, src/burnt_classifier, src/burn_mixing, src/dirty_area, src/acoustic, src/cane_flow, src/plate_ocr, src/cane_on_truck and src/stalk_seg. MIT licensed. GitHub: https://github.com/watcharaponthod-code/sugarcane-cv",
