        content: "ekyc is a monorepo pairing a React Native module with a Python decision server: the phone runs the interaction, the server makes the decision. Layout: packages/react-native-ekyc (the server-verified module the team consumes), packages/react-native-ekyc-local (a 100% on-device variant using ML Kit plus MobileFaceNet with no server), apps/example (an Expo demo of the server flow: enrol, verify, identify), apps/local (an Expo app for the local flow, shipped as a release APK on GitHub Releases), server (a FastAPI decision service using MediaPipe and DeepFace, with an ONNX image on Railway) and docs (design spec, ML validation, PAD evaluation, UI research, device QA). The split is justified in the README: the phone belongs to whoever is holding it, so a rooted device or patched build can make a client-side liveness flag say anything; the phone collects evidence and the server judges it, the same split used by FaceTec, AWS Rekognition Face Liveness, iProov and Onfido. The phone stores no biometric data at all. Vision stack: MediaPipe measures (detection, 478 landmarks, head pose in degrees, eye aspect ratio, blendshapes) and DeepFace identifies (ArcFace embeddings plus the MiniFASNet anti-spoofing ensemble). On the phone, ML Kit produces a real-time signal that only drives the coaching UI; the server never trusts it and re-derives everything from pixels. Setting EKYC_BACKEND=onnx swaps in an ONNX-only stack (SCRFD plus ArcFace plus MiniFASNet) for deployments that cannot carry TensorFlow and PyTorch, and the README states it measures worse. Test counts given: 105 module tests plus 16 local-package tests via npm test; 187 server tests under pytest, 42 of them on real models; status section reports 90 server tests and 77 module tests passing, including enrol to verify to identify to erase end-to-end on real faces. Model download is about 202 MB, SHA-256 verified. Two gates remain before production and are stated as unproven: nothing has run on a physical phone (camera, shutter latency and yawSign calibration need real hardware), and thresholds are calibrated against Western press photography plus one vendor selfie set rather than the target population, with only screen-replay attacks tested — not print, cut-out or mask. Mask defences (openMouth challenge, rPPG pulse) and an ISO/IEC 30107-3 APCER/BPCER harness exist in docs/pad-evaluation.md. Source modules include liveness/LivenessSession.ts, liveness/challenges.ts, quality/sharpness.ts, heavy/pulse.ts and heavy/embedder.ts. GitHub: https://github.com/watcharaponthod-code/ekyc",
