        content: "roblox-dragon-combat publishes excerpts from a live Roblox place: a multiplayer dragon flight-combat game where players fly nose-first through an open sky arena, lock on, and fight with homing fireballs and per-dragon signature skills. The substance of the repo is the bot AI, which is deliberately not a neural network and not a pathfinder. The README gives three reasons reinforcement learning was rejected: the Roblox server runs the brain every Heartbeat for every bot on a budget of a few hundred microseconds, which fits steering math but not policy inference in Luau; the target behaviour is not winning but losing convincingly, which is easier to write as explicit rules than to shape as a reward; and every playtest complaint was local and specific, mapping to a constant that can be moved and retested in a minute. The brain is a finite state machine over steering behaviours with three states: PATROL (waypoints across the arena, 45% of the time a boost ring triggering a 1.4 s surge, altitude change capped at +/-70 studs per waypoint after an earlier +/-240 made bots visibly porpoise), ATTACK (entered when a target is within 1300 studs; aims at target position plus a quarter second of velocity lead, flies through, breaks off at 240 studs banking 75 degrees for 2.2-3.6 s then flips the strafe sign; an earlier 130-stud break produced a tight orbit that read instantly as a machine), and EVADE (below 28% HP with an enemy close, sinusoidal jink, turn rate raised to 200 degrees per second, boost on, 2.4 s before returning). Target selection scores dist plus claimed times 240 so the squad spreads out instead of all diving the one human, and bots count each other as targets. Steering includes a 130-stud whisker ray with +/-45 and +/-80 degree probes, wall slide that strips the into-wall heading component without cutting speed, a soft fence blending toward centre within 420 studs of the edge and inverting outward velocity past it, and a stuck escape checking accumulated movement every 0.7 s against a 35% threshold. Human-likeness tuning: speeds cruise 300 / hunt 360 / boost 600 matching the player controller, turn rate hard capped at 150 degrees per second (down from 273-348), bank up to 72 degrees (old clamp 34), per-bot wobble sin(t*(1.3 + i*0.17) + i), a per-bot BotSkill of 0.5-0.95 driving aim tolerance, fire cooldown and a deliberate 12-40% miss rate, speed easing at dt*4 to match player ACCEL 7.5, and a smoothed heading trailing logic by about 0.15 s. Beatability is engineered: bot fireballs home at 0.45x the player turn rate, skill casts have a 0.75 s visible wind-up, and BotSkill was lowered from 0.7-1.3. Dodge sweeps run at 8 Hz and ring sweeps at 7 Hz. Bots are physics-driven via LinearVelocity and AlignOrientation rather than CFrame writes, so clients interpolate them and the shared animator drives wings from real velocity. Files: src/bot_brain.lua, src/dragon_factory.lua, src/flight_server.lua, src/dragon_animator.lua, src/tail_physics.lua, src/enemy_radar.lua. Not a runnable standalone project. GitHub: https://github.com/watcharaponthod-code/roblox-dragon-combat",
