        content: "subway-kids is a Subway Surfers-style endless runner controlled entirely by body movement in front of a webcam, powered by MediaPipe Pose. The pipeline has six steps: the webcam or browser camera captures live video; frames are preprocessed with a mirror flip and BGR to RGB conversion; MediaPipe Pose in Lite configuration (model_complexity=0) detects 33 body landmarks per frame; the hip centre is computed by averaging the x-coordinates of LEFT_HIP (landmark 23) and RIGHT_HIP (landmark 24); a lane classifier maps that body centre x to a lane using thresholds of below 0.38 for left, 0.38 to 0.62 for centre and above 0.62 for right; and the game engine slides the player to the target lane while obstacles scroll downward at increasing speed. A jump is triggered when the nose y-coordinate goes below 0.3, available in web mode only. Two deployment modes are documented. Local mode runs entirely on the desktop: webcam through OpenCV, into MediaPipe Pose, to a lane value, into Pygame. Web mode sends browser camera frames over a WebSocket to a FastAPI server, which runs MediaPipe Pose and returns lane and jump back to the browser game. Controls are lean left for lane 0, centre for lane 1, lean right for lane 2, with arrow keys as a keyboard fallback, R to restart after game over, F to toggle fullscreen and ESC or Q to quit. The stack is MediaPipe Pose (Lite) for pose detection, OpenCV for computer vision, Pygame running at 60 FPS at 1280x720 resolution, FastAPI with WebSocket for the web server, and Python 3. The repository contains camera.py, game.py, main.py, obstacle.py, player.py and settings.py for the desktop build, a server/ directory with its own camera.py and main.py for the WebSocket service, Dockerfile.server, Dockerfile.web and a docker-compose.yml for containerised deployment, and a web/ directory with a Next.js, Tailwind and React Three Fiber front end (web/app/game/SceneContent.tsx, web/components/GameCanvas.tsx). Installation is pip install -r requirements.txt then python main.py for local mode, or uvicorn main:app --reload inside server/ for web mode. The README documents the mechanism and thresholds but claims no measured accuracy or latency figures. GitHub: https://github.com/watcharaponthod-code/subway-kids",
